Q: Is there an event to detect when user interacted with a page? A: <p>7 year old question, the latest reply was 5 years ago</p>
<p>So, here's how I would tackle it:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function requestFunctionCallAfterUserInteraction(func) {
    if (window.userHasInteracted) {
        func();
    } else {
        const listeners = [&quot;click&quot;, &quot;keydown&quot;, &quot;touchstart&quot;];
        const onInteraction = () =&gt; {
            window.userHasInteracted = true;
            listeners.forEach(listener =&gt; {
                window.removeEventListener(listener, onInteraction);
            });
            func();
        };
        listeners.forEach(listener =&gt; {
            window.addEventListener(listener, onInteraction);
        });
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This should work everywhere (I tested it on Desktop (Chrome, Opera GX, Edge, Firefox) and Android (Opera GX), even in older browsers</p>
<p>And here's a modern approach with <code>navigator.userActivation</code> as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/6697055/tankki3">@Tankki3</a> pointed out:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>async function requestFunctionCallAfterUserInteraction(func, callIfUnsupported = false) {
    if (!navigator.userActivation) {
        console.error(&quot;User activation API not supported&quot;);
        if (callIfUnsupported) func();
        return;
    }
    while (!navigator.userActivation.hasBeenActive) await new Promise(resolve =&gt; setTimeout(resolve, 100));
    func();
}
</code></pre>
<p>After declaring, you can just call it, like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>requestFunctionCallAfterUserInteraction(() =&gt; yourVideo.play());
</code></pre>
<p>Hope it helps somebody</p>

Q: 100% Width of a table not filling the page A: <p>This is what worked for me:</p>
<pre><code>#maintable {
    display: table;
    width: 100%;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Casting in comparator functions for qsort A: <p>There are two imporant remarks according to the comparison function for the standard C function <strong>qsort</strong>. The first one is  (the C24 Standard, section &quot;7.24.5 Searching and sorting utilities&quot;):</p>
<p>2 <strong>The implementation shall ensure that</strong> the second argument of the comparison function (when called from bsearch), or <strong>both arguments (when called from qsort), are pointers to elements of the array</strong>. The first argument when called from bsearch shall equal key.</p>
<p>And the second one is</p>
<p>3 <strong>The comparison function shall not alter the contents of the array</strong>. <strong>The implementation may reorder elements of the array between calls to the comparison function, but shall not alter the contents of any individual element.</strong></p>
<p>The comparison function itself is declared the following way</p>
<pre><code>int compar(const void *a, const void *b);
</code></pre>
<p>So if elements of an array has the type <strong>T *^n arr[]</strong> then within the comparison funtion you need to cast the direferenced pointer to <strong>const void</strong> <strong>a</strong> or <strong>b</strong> like</p>
<pre><code>T *^n const element = *( T *^n const * )a;
</code></pre>
<p>Or you may even write</p>
<pre><code>T *^n const element = *( T *^n const ( * ) )a;
</code></pre>
<p>That is the pointer to the incomplete constant type <strong>const</strong> <strong>void</strong> must be casted to a pointer to the constant element type <strong>const <em>array_element_type</em></strong>.  It means that you must qualify the passed by pointers elements of an array as constant elements. If the array element type has a pointer type then within the comparison function is shall be considered as a constant pointer to the array element type</p>
<p>Let's consider the following demonstration program.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

int string_cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    const char * const s1 = *( const char * const * )a;
    const char * const s2 = *( const char * const * )b;

    return strcmp( s1, s2 );
}

int main(void) 
{
    const char *s[] = { &quot;World&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot; };
    const size_t N = sizeof( s ) / sizeof( *s );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( s, N, sizeof( *s ), string_cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output is</p>
<pre><code>World Hello 
Hello World 
</code></pre>
<p>In the above program elements of the array are pointers. So within the comparison function they are processed as constant pointers. You can rewrite the program the following way.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

typedef const char * T;

int string_cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    const T s1 = *( T const * )a;
    const T s2 = *( T const * )b;

    return strcmp( s1, s2 );
}

int main(void) 
{
    T s[] = { &quot;World&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot; };
    const size_t N = sizeof( s ) / sizeof( *s );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( s, N, sizeof( *s ), string_cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%s &quot;, s[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have for example an array of an arithmetic type then you can write</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

int cmp( const void *a, const void *b )
{
    int x = *( int const * )a;
    int y = *( int const * )b;

    return ( y &lt; x ) - ( x &lt; y );
}

int main(void) 
{
    int a[] = { 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 };
    const size_t N = sizeof( a ) / sizeof( *a );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%d &quot;, a[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

    qsort( a, N, sizeof( *a ), cmp );

    for ( size_t i = 0; i &lt; N; i++ )
    {
        printf( &quot;%d &quot;, a[i] );
    }
    putchar( '\n' );

}
</code></pre>
<p>The program output is</p>
<pre><code>5 4 3 2 1 
1 2 3 4 5
</code></pre>
<p>Within the comparison function the record of the casting type as for example in this line</p>
<pre><code>int x = *( int const * )a;
</code></pre>
<p>is equivalent to</p>
<pre><code>int x = *( const int * )a;
</code></pre>
